{"kind":"request","text":"Decide the max-fan-out target against the metric it should measure, then meet it.","body":{"conditions":"Measured at main adda28c8 (docs/reference/performance.md): appending a record resting on 256 bases takes about 774 ms against the 50 ms target this work adopted, so the target as written is missed by about fifteen times at both envelopes. But the same workroom appends a record resting on a single base in about 764 ms, so almost all of that is the fixed cost of an append — signing, writing and folding — and almost none of it is fan-out. Measured on the harness fan-out axis across 1, 16, 64 and 256 bases at depth 50,000, the incremental cost is 0.22 ms per added base; codex measured 0.037 ms per added base at depth 100. The dimension the target was meant to protect against, fan-out making one record expensive, does not appear at any width up to the FIRST-PRODUCTION maximum of 256. This request therefore asks for a judgement before any optimization: decide whether the 50 ms target should be restated against incremental per-base cost, which the implementation already meets by two orders of magnitude, or kept as an absolute per-append budget, which would make it a request to reduce the fixed cost of an append rather than anything to do with fan-out. Satisfied when the target is restated with its reasoning recorded, and either met or replaced by a bounded request against the fixed append cost, without weakening any signing or fold invariant.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}